If you are using X-windows with normal Xterms, you can add the
following to your .Xresources or .Xdefaults (which ever you use) to
get the numberpad key to work in each X-term:

XTerm*VT100.translations:       \
#override \
    <Key>Num_Lock: keymap(numberpadlock) \n

XTerm*VT100.numberpadlockKeymap.translations:   \
#override \
    <Key>Num_Lock:   keymap(None) \n\
    <Key>KP_Add: string("+") \n\
    <Key>F9:   string("[D") \n\
    <Key>F11:   string("[A") \n\
    <Key>F10:   string("[B") \n\
    <Key>F12:   string("[C") \n\
    <Key>F24:   string("=") \n\
    <Key>F25:   string("/") \n\
    <Key>KP_Divide:   string("/") \n\
    <Key>F26:   string("*") \n\
    <Key>KP_Multiply:   string("*") \n\
    <Key>KP_Subtract:   string("-") \n\
    <Key>KP_Decimal:   string(".") \n\
    <Key>KP_0:   string("0") \n\
    <Key>Insert:   string("0") \n\
    <Key>KP_1:   string("1") \n\
    <Key>R13:   string("1") \n\
    <Key>KP_2:   string("2") \n\
    <Key>Down:   string("2") \n\
    <Key>KP_3:   string("3") \n\
    <Key>F35:   string("3") \n\
    <Key>KP_4:   string("4") \n\
    <Key>Left:   string("4") \n\
    <Key>KP_5:   string("5") \n\
    <Key>F31:   string("5") \n\
    <Key>KP_6:   string("6") \n\
    <Key>Right:   string("6") \n\
    <Key>KP_7:   string("7") \n\
    <Key>F27:   string("7") \n\
    <Key>KP_8:   string("8") \n\
    <Key>KP_9:   string("9") \n\
    <Key>F29:   string("9") \n

This will remap the numberpad keys in an xterm to output numbers when
you press the Num_Lock key. It will also return the keypad to it's
original values when you press the Num_Lock key again so that the
arrow keys will work (in an editor for example). As to what you have
to do for the cmdtool which is supplied with openwindows, I don't
know.

